 Evaluating one claim costs $O(|\mathcal{S}(c)|\cdot\max|S|+|R|+|D|)$ plus lineage depth. Propagation from a rejected record visits its lineage closure, the claims and assertions linked to closure members, and (by scan) the decisions citing them; this scan is the source of the $O(D)$ propagation cost visible at $10^5$ triples.
 
+\subsection{Operational construct boundary}
+The state derivation above is deterministic only \emph{after} the relation structure has been supplied. The implementation does not infer whether an evidence record is scientifically sufficient, whether a premise is genuinely necessary, whether two apparent alternatives are statistically independent, or whether a manuscript span is correctly bound to a claim. We therefore distinguish \emph{representation semantics} from \emph{construct acquisition}. The former is tested by SUP, REG, SM-CONF and TMS-XC; the latter requires independent annotation and is the primary unresolved target of Section~S7. In particular, ``live,'' ``supported'' and ``admissible'' are ledger-relative workflow labels, not truth or credibility judgements.
